#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:1
msgid "How your records become tax figures"
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:3
msgid ""
"This page covers where the numbers on your tax form come from: how a "
"plain bank movement turns into a figure for each numbered box on an "
"official form, and why the tool is built to let you explain every figure "
"later. It walks through the idea, not the buttons - the how-to guides "
"linked from each section carry the commands."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:9
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The forms in question are modelos that you send to the {term}`AEAT`. Each"
" modelo is made of {term}`casillas <casilla>`. Your job, with the tool's "
"help, is to put the right figure in each box and to keep the evidence "
"behind it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:11
msgid "A bank movement means nothing on its own"
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:13
msgid ""
"A line on a bank statement is just a date and an amount. The money moved,"
" but the statement doesn't know whether it was a customer paying you, a "
"supplier you paid, your weekly groceries, or a transfer between your own "
"accounts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:15
msgid ""
"Tax meaning isn't in the bank data. You add it. Until you do, a movement "
"can't count toward any box on any form."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:17
msgid "Making a record tax-ready"
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:19
msgid ""
"To give a movement tax meaning, you turn it into a sorted, categorized "
"record. A tax-ready record carries three things:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:21
msgid "a business, personal, or mixed decision;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:22
msgid "a category, such as customer sales or office supplies;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:23
msgid ""
"and, for a mixed cost, the business-versus-personal split, so only the "
"business share counts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:25
msgid ""
"A record that has all of this is ready to feed into a calculation. To do "
"this for your own movements, see [Classify transactions](../how-to"
"/classify-transactions.md)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:27
msgid "Splitting a mixed cost, and checking readiness"
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:29
msgid ""
"Some costs serve both your business and your private life - a phone bill,"
" home internet, a home office. Only the business share counts, and the "
"split can come from a percentage on the single record, a saved default "
"for the whole category, or a ratio worked out from your registered facts "
"- see [the mixed-use workflow](../how-to/classify-transactions.md"
"#classify-mixed-use-transactions)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:35
msgid ""
"Before the tool adds anything up, a readiness check flags each record in "
"the period that is still missing a decision, a category, a tax figure, a "
"split reference, or a convertible currency. The check changes nothing; it"
" tells you which records are not ready so you fix the raw material before"
" trusting any total - see the readiness section of [Import and manage "
"transactions](../how-to/import-bank-statements.md)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:42
msgid "Which form, and which period"
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:44
msgid ""
"A calculation is always for one form, one year, and one period. The "
"period decides which of your records count."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:46
msgid ""
"A period is a quarter, a single month, or a whole year. The tool turns it"
" into a start and end date, then keeps only the records whose date falls "
"inside that window. A first-quarter filing sees January through March; a "
"March filing sees only March."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:48
msgid ""
"Choosing the right form for your activity is its own decision - see [Find"
" out which modelos apply to you](../how-to/choose-modelo.md). For how "
"quarters, months, and annual periods map to dates, see [Period tokens and"
" dates](../how-to/filing-calendar.md#period-tokens-and-dates)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:50
msgid "From records to numbered boxes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:52
msgid ""
"Here's where the figures appear. The tool reads two things: your tax-"
"ready records for the period, and your profile - who you are and what you"
" do."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:54
msgid ""
"It then applies the rules the agency publishes for that form. Those rules"
" decide which input feeds which box, and how the boxes relate to each "
"other. A cost you marked as office supplies lands in the box for that "
"kind of expense. Income lands in its own box. From there, the rules add, "
"subtract, and apply rates - total income minus deductible costs to reach "
"a net figure, a rate applied to reach the tax due - until every box on "
"the form holds a figure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:56
msgid ""
"You don't wire any of this by hand. You make your records tax-ready and "
"keep your profile current; the published rules do the routing and the "
"arithmetic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:58
msgid "Tracing a number back to the law"
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:60
msgid ""
"Every figure the tool produces keeps three things attached: the rule that"
" produced it, the law article behind that rule, and the section of the "
"official manual that explains it. Input figures you entered yourself "
"carry the same trail back to their source."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:62
msgid ""
"This is the point of the whole design. Spanish tax filing expects you to "
"justify every number. If an inspector asks why a box holds a certain "
"figure, you can show the records behind it and the rule and law that "
"turned those records into that figure. Nothing is a black box."
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:64
msgid "Where this sits in the journey"
msgstr ""

#: ../../explanation/from-records-to-figures.md:66
msgid ""
"This page is part of understanding the AEAT pipeline - how your data "
"flows from bank statement to filed form."
msgstr ""

